<header class="page-hero">
  <div class="shell page-hero-grid">
    <div><p class="eyebrow"><span></span> About the project</p><h1>A Lean 4 library for mathematical economics.</h1></div>
    <p>{{ site.name }} develops definitions and proofs as Lean 4 code. Researchers can import them into new models, reuse proved results, and have Lean check new theorems.</p>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="page-section section-rule">
  <div class="shell editorial-grid">
    <div class="sticky-label">Our purpose</div>
    <div class="prose-large">
      <h2>Formal definitions expose assumptions.</h2>
      <p>Economic theorems depend on precise choices about agents, information, incentives, distributions, and equilibrium. Formalizing those choices makes the relationships among results visible and allows Lean's small trusted kernel to check each proof.</p>
      <p>Researchers can reuse earlier formalized definitions and theorems, compare the hypotheses required by different models, and state precisely what changes in a new result.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="page-section section-rule soft-section">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="section-heading compact-heading"><p class="kicker">Current architecture</p><h2>Three connected levels</h2></div>
    <div class="architecture-flow">
      <article><span>01</span><h3>Mechanism</h3><p>The rules: allocation <code>x</code> and payment <code>t</code> over reports.</p></article>
      <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
      <article><span>02</span><h3>Auction</h3><p>The rules plus types, bounds, distributions, and a probability space.</p></article>
      <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
      <article><span>03</span><h3>BidderProblem</h3><p>One bidder’s interim allocation, cost, utility, and strategy.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="page-section section-rule">
  <div class="shell editorial-grid">
    <div class="sticky-label">Design commitments</div>
    <div class="value-list">
      <article><span>01</span><div><h3>Economic objects stay recognizable</h3><p>Definitions use the language of allocations, payments, types, utility, strategies, and seller objectives.</p></div></article>
      <article><span>02</span><div><h3>Assumptions compose</h3><p>Independent types, identical types, bounded support, density conditions, and incentive notions are expressed separately and combined when needed.</p></div></article>
      <article><span>03</span><div><h3>General theory supports new results</h3><p>Reusable single-parameter results feed new analyses of auctions, posted prices, and welfare.</p></div></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
